2.2.m:
Having no other refuge, O Lord, take pity on me, O omnipresent one!"
While praising thus joyfully near the lotus feet of Jagannātha,
he fell to the ground like a stick, saying repeatedly, "Be gracious!"
Then all those deities, having praised and worshiped Keśava,
delighted by the playful side-glance of his, departed once again to heaven.
Then, their eyes opened, Puṇḍarīka and Ambarīṣa,
deluded by the illusory power of Viṣṇu, thought that what they had seen was a dream.
For, having seen that divine play directly with their fleshy eyes,
the two of them, having returned to human consciousness, [saw] him seated upon a divine throne:
resembling a dark-blue raincloud, with large eyes like blossoming lotuses;
with crimson lips, a beautiful nose, adorned with divine earrings;
bearing the conch, discus, mace, and lotus, wearing a garland of forest flowers;
with a broad chest, a lovely necklace, radiant with a priceless crown;
his chest marked by the Śrīvatsa and Kaustubha, adorned with divine armlets;
with long-reaching arms, intent upon protecting the wretched and afflicted;
fastened with a golden cord having a jewel at the center knot;
